I wrote some unit tests for the normal distro for proportional-to
probability calcs.  The pattern I used below should work for
all the distros and help simplify writing the tests.

The tests are to ensure that the proportionality should
be the same in the full calc (template propto parameter = false)
and the propto calc (template propto parameter = true).

I used a single templated helper function for the normal
distribution and an overloaded equality test function.

I then went through all the combinations of constant and
variable arguments and tested each (holding the constant
part constant and varying the variable part, naturally).

We should be able to reuse the overloaded helper function
expect_eq_diffs, so it should be pulled out into some file
that's included from the other tests.

- Bob

------------------------

using stan::prob::normal_log;
using stan::is_constant;
using stan::agrad::var;

void expect_eq_diffs(double x1, double x2,
		     double y1, double y2) {
   EXPECT_FLOAT_EQ(x1-x2,y1-y2);
}

void expect_eq_diffs(var x1, var x2,
		     var y1, var y2) {
   expect_eq_diffs(x1.val(), x2.val(),
		  y1.val(), y2.val());
}

template <typename T_y, typename T_loc, typename T_scale>
void expect_propto(T_y y1, T_loc mu1, T_scale sigma1,
		   T_y y2, T_loc mu2, T_scale sigma2) {
   expect_eq_diffs(normal_log<false>(y1,mu1,sigma1),
		  normal_log<false>(y2,mu2,sigma2),
		  normal_log<true>(y1,mu1,sigma1),
		  normal_log<true>(y2,mu2,sigma2));
}

TEST(distributionsNormal,propto) {
   // d,d,d
   expect_propto(1.0,2.0,3.0,
		1.0,2.0,3.0);
   // d,d,v
   expect_propto(1.0,2.0,var(3.0),
		1.0,2.0,var(4.0));
   // d,v,d
   expect_propto(1.0,var(2.0),3.0,
		1.0,var(4.0),3.0);
   // d,v,v
   expect_propto(1.0,var(2.0),var(3.0),
		1.0,var(-1.0),var(1.0));
   // v,d,d
   expect_propto(var(1.0),2.0,3.0,
		var(3.0),2.0,3.0);
   // v,d,v
   expect_propto(var(1.0),2.0,var(3.0),
		var(4.0),2.0,var(1.0));
   // v,v,d
   expect_propto(var(1.0),var(2.0),3.0,
		var(2.0),var(-4.0),3.0);
   // v,v,v
   expect_propto(var(1.0),var(2.0),var(3.0),
		var(5.0),var(7.0),var(1.5));
}

- Bob

I just added a file:

     src/stan/prob/traits.hpp

It contains a single metaprogram to calculate whether or
not to use a summand in a probability calculation.

Given Daniel's factoring of error policy and tests and
our inclusion of propto as a boolean param, we've now reduced
the normal density to a single manageable and comprehensible
function!  Here's the code, which includes examples of
the propto metaprogram in use:

     template <bool propto = false,
	      typename T_y, typename T_loc, typename T_scale,
	      class Policy = policy<> >

     inline typename promote_args<T_y,T_loc,T_scale>::type

     normal_log(const T_y& y, const T_loc& mu, const T_scale& sigma,
	       const Policy& = Policy()) {

       static const char* function = "stan::prob::normal_log<%1%>(%1%)";

       typename promote_args<T_y,T_loc,T_scale>::type lp(0.0);

       if (!check_scale(function, sigma, &lp, Policy()))
	return lp;

       if (!check_location(function, mu, &lp, Policy()))
	return lp;

       if (!check_x(function, y, &lp, Policy()))
	return lp;

       if (include_summand<propto,T_y,T_loc,T_scale>::value)
	lp -= square(y - mu) / (2.0 * square(sigma));

       if (include_summand<propto,T_scale>::value)
	lp -= log(sigma);

       if (include_summand<propto>::value)
	lp += NEG_LOG_SQRT_TWO_PI;

       return lp;
     }

Metaprogramming's the bomb once you get your head around it.
I also used it to shrink the tests to a manageable size.

And check out Boost's groovy trick on the policy arg.  Because
Policy is always a sizeless struct, this basically lets the
compiler ignore all the passing of Policy args and manage everything
statically through the types.  (It took Daniel and I a while to
even figure out what this pattern from Boost was doing.)

- Bob

After the really useful discussion Wednesday, I'd like to add one
more  kind of variable, a derived quantity computed once per sample.
I'll also rename "derived" to "transformed", leaving us with
the following six blocks per Stan program, all of which are
optional other than model and parameters.

// read once per chain (double)
data { declarations }

// computed once per chain (double)
transformed data { declarations statements }

// read once per leapfrog step with transforms (auto-dif var)
parameters { declarations }

// computed per leapfrog step (auto-dif var)
transformed parameters { declarations statements }

// computed once per leapfrog step (auto-dif var)
model { statements }

// computed once per saved sample (double)
derived quantities { declarations statements }

I indicated how often each of the block of statements
are run and the type of variables they run over (auto-dif
is much more expensive).  Computing the log probabilty
(including log Jacobian determinants of transforms) and
its gradient will dominate -- it's most expense and computed
per leapfrog step.

I'll add some stricter error testing, like transformed
parameters on the left-hand-side of a sample or
actual parameters on the left-hand-side of an assignment
and flag them as warnings by default.

- Bob

I added two mixture model examples, one for sampling
a single value and one for estimation.

They could be defined by:

     z ~ bernoulli(theta); // z in { 0, 1 }
     y ~ normal(mu[z+1],sigma[z+1]);  // index in { 1, 2 }

but our discrete sampling won't be great, at least
to start.

We'll do better to just sum out z.  What we'll get is

     y ~ theta * normal(mu[1],sigma[1])
         + (1 - theta) * normal(mu[2],sigma[2])

To encode this in Stan, we add the cacluated log probability
of y to the running total.

src/models/basic_distributions/normal_mixture.stan
---------------------------------------------------
derived data {
   double(0,1) theta;
   double mu[2];
   double(0,) sigma[2];

   mu[1] <- 0.0;    sigma[1] <- 0.5;
   mu[2] <- 4.0;    sigma[2] <- 3.0;
   theta <- 0.25;
}
parameters {
   double y;
}
model {
   lp__ <- lp__
        + log_sum_exp(log(theta) + normal_log(y,mu[1],sigma[1]),
                      log(1.0 - theta) + normal_log(y,mu[2],sigma[2])));
}
------------------------------------------------------

It works pretty well (densities estimated from 1000 draws have
roughly the right bimodal shape anyway).

We use log_sum_exp(a,b) for managing overflow.  It's
defined by

   log_sum_exp(a,b) = log(exp(a) + exp(b))

and the trick for managing overflow is to let m = max(a,b)
and use

   log_sum_exp(a,b) = m + log(exp(a-m) + exp(b-m))

If you unwind the above example, you get the following
term for the log probability:

   log (theta * normal(y|mu[1],sigma[1])
        + (1 - theta) * normal(y|mu[2],sigma[2]) )

There's also an array version, log_sum_exp(vector<double>),
defied by the obvious extension.

I also included an estimator version with fixed variances
for convenience:

    src/models/basic_estimators/normal_mixture.stan

- Bob

